            "bodyHtml": "<div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png\" width=\"489\" height=\"489\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1024,&quot;width&quot;:1024,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:489,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:null,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:true,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!gj87!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F8792dee0-d695-40f4-af31-fdbd17d14d0f_1024x1024.png 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" fetchpriority=\"high\"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p><em>Saturday, April 18, 2026, 00:49am</em></p><p>During a training session conducted by Gino Terentin, I was introduced to a point that often goes unnoticed in the work routine: the impact of consecutive meetings on cognitive performance.</p><p>A study of the <a href=\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/brain-research#:~:text=1.,words%2C%20the%20stress%20kept%20accumulating.\">Microsoft Research</a>, using EEG, observed that consecutive meetings generate an accumulation of brain stress (beta waves), without sufficient time for dissipation between them. As a result, the brain already starts the next meeting in a compromised state.</p><p>This effect is not necessarily related to the complexity of the meeting, but to the <strong>lack of recovery breaks</strong>.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>Simple adjustments with relevant impact</strong></p><p>Small changes in the way you organize your agenda can help reduce this effect.</p><p><strong>1. Create automatic calendar buffers</strong></p><p>An effective practice is to configure the calendar so that meetings end before the full time.</p><ul><li><p>30-minute meetings now last 25 minutes</p></li><li><p>60-minute meetings now last 50 minutes</p></li></ul><p>This automatically creates a break between meetings without relying on manual discipline.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>2. Perform physical reset between meetings</strong></p><p>The break between meetings doesn't have to be productive in the traditional sense.</p><ul><li><p>Raise</p></li><li><p>Drink water</p></li><li><p>Look out the window</p></li></ul><p>The idea is to avoid continuous mental activity. The most effective reset, in this context, is <strong>physical</strong>, non-cognitive.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>3. Allow cognitive recovery time</strong></p><p>In addition to short breaks, it is recommended to block out a longer period of time throughout the day.</p><ul><li><p>A 30-minute slot may be enough</p></li><li><p>Preferably in the middle of the day</p></li></ul><p>This time is not idle. It acts as maintenance of cognitive capacity throughout the rest of the agenda.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>4. Adjust driving when there is no gap</strong></p><p>In scenarios where back-to-back meetings are unavoidable, especially for those who schedule or lead:</p><ul><li><p>Avoid starting the next meeting immediately</p></li><li><p>Reserve 1 to 2 initial minutes for transition</p></li><li><p>Use a simple question or brief silence to “ground” the group</p></li></ul><p>This small adjustment helps reduce accumulated overhead.</p><div><hr></div><p><strong>Final consideration</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The problem with consecutive meetings is related to volume and the lack of transition between them. Simple adjustments to the agenda structure can improve the quality of interactions and decision-making capacity throughout the day, without the need for more complex structural changes.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Yours sincerely,<br>Mateus Lucena</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"></p>"
